VERDICTPartly correct: the mathematical logic is perfect, but the code crashes on small inputs like n = 0 or n = 1 because it tries to access array indices that do not exist.REVIEW MY CODEWhat I did well.You correctly implemented the Sieve of Eratosthenes, which is exactly the right algorithm for this problem. You also knew to start your inner loop at i * i, which is a great time-saving trick.Is it correct? If not, give me a failing test case, point to the exact line(s), and show only the fix.It is not entirely correct because it crashes with a memory out-of-bounds error on small numbers.Failing test case: n = 0.Exact lines causing the issue:prime[0]=false;prime[1]=false;When n is 0, n + 1 is 1. The array only has a single slot at index 0, so asking to modify prime[1] causes the program to crash.The fix is to add a safety guard at the very top of your function:C++if (n <= 2) return 0;
Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses.Because the problem asks for the count of primes strictly less than n, the answer for n = 0, n = 1, and n = 2 is always 0. Your code misses the safety check for sizes less than 2.Additionally, for very huge inputs, j = i * i could overflow a standard 32-bit integer, turning into a negative number and causing an infinite loop or a crash. Using a larger data type like long long for j prevents this, though it happens to pass on LeetCode because their maximum n for this problem is small enough.Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason.Time complexity: $O(n \log \log n)$ because crossing out multiples of primes takes fewer and fewer steps as the primes get larger.Space complexity: $O(n)$ because you allocate a boolean vector of size n + 1.Why my approach works, or why it doesn't.Your approach works because instead of testing every single number with slow division to see if it has factors, you start with the lowest prime numbers and use fast addition to proactively cross off their multiples. Whatever is left uncrossed must be a prime number.ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISEDApproach 1: Check Every Number (Brute Force)Where it comes from: Common beginner intuition.Idea in simple words: Go through every number from 2 up to n. For each number, check if it can be divided by any smaller number up to its square root.C++ code:C++class Solution {
private:
    bool isPrime(int num) {
        if (num <= 1) return false;
        for (int i = 2; i * i <= num; i++) {
            if (num % i == 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
public:
    int countPrimes(int n) {
        int count = 0;
        for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {
            if (isPrime(i)) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
};
Time and space complexity: Time is $O(n \sqrt{n})$ because checking each number takes up to the square root of that number in steps. Space is $O(1)$ because it only uses a few counter variables.What makes the next approach better: Doing division on every single number is very slow. We can use addition to jump across multiples instead.Approach 2: Sieve of Eratosthenes (Optimal)Where it comes from: Ancient Greek mathematics, and the standard expected answer for this problem.Idea in simple words: Create a list of all numbers up to n. Start at 2, count it as prime, and cross out every multiple of 2. Move to the next uncrossed number, which is 3, count it, and cross out its multiples. Repeat this to filter out all non-primes.C++ code:See section 6 below.Time and space complexity: Time is $O(n \log \log n)$ due to the mathematical progression of prime multiples. Space is $O(n)$ to store the true/false list.What makes the next approach better: This is generally the best approach for an interview. You can do tiny micro-optimizations like completely ignoring even numbers to cut the memory usage in half, but the core idea and time complexity remain exactly the same.WHERE I STANDWhich approach did I use?You used Approach 2, the Sieve of Eratosthenes.Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem?An interviewer expects exactly the Sieve of Eratosthenes. They also expect you to handle the n = 0 and n = 1 edge cases gracefully without crashing.Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:Question 1: What if n is so massive that the boolean array does not fit in the computer's memory?Answer: You would use a Segmented Sieve. You break the huge range into smaller chunks that fit easily in RAM, find the base primes up to the square root of n, and use those base primes to cross out multiples in one small chunk at a time.Question 2: Why do you start the inner crossing-out loop at i * i instead of i * 2?Answer: Because any multiple smaller than i * i, such as i * 2 or i * 3, would have already been crossed out when we processed the smaller prime numbers like 2 and 3.WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBERThe key insight, in one sentence:Instead of doing expensive division to check if numbers are prime, use fast addition to cross out the multiples of known primes.My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time:Always check if your input is large enough before hardcoding array assignments like index 1.Watch out for integer overflow when multiplying i by i in loop conditions.The pattern or idea this question is really about:The Sieve of Eratosthenes and array-based state tracking.THE BEST SOLUTIONC++class Solution {
public:
    int countPrimes(int n) {
        if (n <= 2) {
            return 0;
        }

        vector<bool> prime(n, true);
        prime[0] = false;
        prime[1] = false;

        for (int i = 2; i * i < n; i++) {
            if (prime[i]) {
                for (long long j = (long long)i * i; j < n; j += i) {
                    prime[j] = false;
                }
            }
        }

        int count = 0;
        for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {
            if (prime[i]) {
                count++;
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
};
